<https://example.org/amltrix/link/ae5eef78-57ca-45c6-b91d-8504f1650456> a amlo:ActorLink ;
    dct:description """Financial institutions are exploited when:

- Criminals present separate but identical invoices to different branches or departments, each believing it is financing a distinct transaction.
- Letters of credit, loans, or other credit lines are approved based on duplicate trade documentation, unwittingly funding a single shipment multiple times.

This fragmentation among multiple financial providers obscures the money trail and amplifies the layering of illicit proceeds."""@en ;
    amlo:actor amx:AT0083 ;
    amlo:technique amx:T0008.001 .

<https://example.org/amltrix/link/ae666040-4c44-4146-a9da-f59cb4ebea71> a amlo:ProductServiceLink ;
    dct:description """- Criminals convert proceeds from diamond sales into multiple currencies, obscuring the trail of funds.  
- Repeated or structured exchanges across different jurisdictions create layering that hinders law enforcement tracking."""@en ;
    amlo:productService amx:PS0106 ;
    amlo:technique amx:T0048.001 .

<https://example.org/amltrix/link/ae76aaab-d8d9-486f-96af-e0a2f07ba5a0> a amlo:ProductServiceLink ;
    dct:description """- Criminals submit fictitious shipping records or invoices to obtain financing for goods purportedly in transit, although no actual shipment exists.  
