<https://example.org/amltrix/link/ce8bc33f-35be-411e-a0b4-447ae61ea0e5> a amlo:ProductServiceLink ;
    dct:description """- Criminals exploit lock-and-mint bridging to create new tokens on one chain in exchange for locked tokens on another, breaking direct transactional links and obscuring origin.  
- The decentralized nature of bridging often bypasses conventional KYC measures, enabling illicit funds to move freely across multiple blockchain networks without a clear paper trail.  
- Large collateral pools held by bridges can facilitate high-value laundering, allowing criminals to embed large sums in ostensibly legitimate liquidity flows.  
- Integrating bridging with minimal-KYC wallets or other decentralized platforms further complicates investigations, impeding authorities’ ability to trace transactions across disparate chains."""@en ;
    amlo:productService amx:PS0049 ;
    amlo:technique amx:T0005.002 .

<https://example.org/amltrix/link/ce93bce5-0d40-4204-b892-a06faed784ee> a amlo:MitigationLink ;
